        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_016ZCtwyHxFuzDhQxchWUFpN",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: How centralizing credential scoping and runtime-path resolution at a single composition point prevents security and configuration bugs across the system.\\ntimestamp: 2024-12-19T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Composition\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly describe their respective decisions as centralized at a single point; this synthesis connects them as instances of the same architectural principle.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-06-19\\n---\\n\\nGrimoire\u0027s architecture centralizes two distinct categories of runtime initialization\\nat a single composition point \u2014 the Hub\u0027s startup path \u2014 rather than scattering either\\ndecision across the codebase.\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] centralizes how secrets reach agent processes: a local secrets\\nloader reads from the \u0060.env\u0060 file and injects credentials only into each spawned child\\nprocess\u0027s environment, never into the Hub\u0027s own process. This prevents any compromised\\nor misbehaving part of the Hub from accessing the credential by reading its own\\nenvironment.\\n\\n[[runtime-paths]] centralizes where all file-system locations are resolved: a single\\npath resolver reads configuration at startup, validates every required input exists,\\nand publishes the resolved location set to all downstream code. Nothing else in the\\ncodebase rediscovers locations or reads the ambient working directory directly.\\n\\nBoth decisions follow the same principle: **one, guarded composition point; everything\\nelse consumes the already-resolved state.** This is more than a style choice. Spreading\\nsecrets-injection logic across multiple spawn sites would multiply the attack surface\\nfor credential leaks. Scattering path resolution across the codebase would make it easy\\nto accidentally use an unvalidated path or inherit a location from a misconfigured\\nworking directory. By doing both at one place, the Hub gains a single, auditable\\ncheckpoint where security and configuration assumptions are made concrete before any\\nagent code runs.\\n\\nWhen adding a new agent, a new secret, or a new data directory \u2014 you extend that one\\nresolver, not a dozen places in the code.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
