      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are both instances of the same architectural principle: **centralizing initialization at a single composition point**.\n\n[[credential-scoping]] centralizes how secrets reach processes: a secrets loader reads the Anthropic API key from a git-ignored \u0060.env\u0060 file at startup and injects it only into each agent\u0027s child-process environment, never into the Hub\u0027s own process. This creates a single, guarded checkpoint where the credential can be audited before leaving the Hub.\n\n[[runtime-paths]] centralizes where all file-system locations are resolved: a single path resolver reads configuration at startup, validates every required input exists, and publishes the resolved location set to all downstream code. Nothing else rediscovers paths or reads the ambient working directory.\n\nBoth decisions follow the same pattern for the same reason: **doing both at one place prevents the bugs and security issues that come from scattering that logic.** If secrets-injection happened at multiple spawn sites, you\u0027d multiply the attack surface for credential leaks. If path resolution happened across the codebase, you\u0027d easily miss an unvalidated path or accidentally inherit a misconfigured working directory. By centralizing both at the Hub\u0027s startup, you gain one auditable checkpoint where security and configuration assumptions are concrete before any agent code runs.\n\nWhen you add a new agent, a new secret, or a new data directory, you extend that one resolver \u2014 you don\u0027t patch a dozen places in the code.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]].",
